        {
          "title": "Inventory Management",
          "subtitle": "Jul 2024 - Jul 2024",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Key Features:\nAdd & Remove Items: Quickly update your inventory by adding or removing items with just a click.\nReal-Time Updates: All changes are instantly reflected, ensuring your inventory is always up-to-date.\nUser-Friendly Interface: Clean, intuitive design that makes navigation and management a breeze.\nCustomizable: Easily tailor the app to suit your specific inventory needs.\n🔧 Technology Stack:\nFrontend: Built with React.js for a dynamic and responsive user experience.\nBackend: Firebase Firestore for secure, real-time data storage and retrieval.\nUI Components: Styled with Material-UI for a modern look and feel.\nWhether you're keeping track of office supplies, warehouse stock, or just want to stay organized at home, our Inventory Tracker app is your go-to solution."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Automating Test Case Generation for Object-Oriented Testing Using the Hybrid Cuckoo and Simulated Annealing Algorithms",
          "subtitle": "Sep 2023 - Apr 2024",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with University of Greenwich"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Project Overview\nThe goal of this project was to develop a hybrid metaheuristic algorithm that combines the explorative capabilities of Cuckoo Search (CS) with the exploitative precision of Simulated Annealing (SA). This hybrid approach aims to tackle the challenges of testing OOP software by ensuring comprehensive code coverage and robust fault detection while adhering to the principles of OOP.\n\nKey Features\n1. Hybrid Algorithm Implementation: Uses Cuckoo Search for global exploration and Simulated Annealing for local optimization.\n2. Robust Fitness Function: Custom fitness function designed to evaluate test cases against multiple criteria including code coverage, fault detection, and execution efficiency.\n3. GUI Application: A user-friendly graphical interface that allows both technical and non-technical users to easily generate and manage test cases.\n4. Comprehensive Testing: Includes tests for key OOP features such as polymorphism, encapsulation, and inheritance."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Content-Based Recommender Systems Analysis",
          "subtitle": "Sep 2022 - Dec 2022",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with University of Greenwich"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "I was involved in a project examining content-based recommender systems, comparing them with collaborative filtering techniques using datasets from Netflix and MovieLens. The study focused on utilizing TF-IDF vectorization and cosine similarity to provide personalized content recommendations efficiently.\n\nKey Achievements:\n\nDeveloped a recommender system prototype that effectively suggests content by analyzing user preferences and item features.\nConducted comprehensive experiments to benchmark the performance (execution time and accuracy) against collaborative filtering methods.\nGained insights into the scalability and applicability of different recommender algorithms in real-world scenarios.\nTechnologies Used:\nPython, pandas, numpy, scikit-learn.\n\nThis project enhanced my understanding of machine learning applications in content recommendation, contributing to more targeted and user-friendly browsing experiences. For more details on this research, feel free to connect or reach out to me directly."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Nutritional Supplements Impact Analysis on Lab Animals Using R",
          "subtitle": "Sep 2022 - Nov 2022",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with University of Greenwich"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "I'm thrilled to share my project on \"Nutritional Supplements Impact Analysis on Lab Animals Using R,\" completed as part of my coursework at the University of Greenwich. This report delves into the significant effects of nutritional treatments on 200 rats and 200 mice, employing a robust hypothesis testing framework to validate the outcomes scientifically.\n\nKey Highlights:\n\n1. Utilized R to generate and analyze experimental data, employing both parametric and non-parametric tests.\n2. Demonstrated rigorous statistical methods, including t-tests and Shapiro-Wilk tests, to ensure the validity of the research results.\n3. Successfully identified the appropriate statistical distributions for analyzing treatment effects, enhancing the scientific understanding of nutritional impacts on animal health.\n4. This project not only honed my skills in statistical analysis and hypothesis testing but also reinforced the critical role of data-driven decisions in biological research. I'm looking forward to applying these skills in future projects!\n\n#DataScience #RProgramming #StatisticalAnalysis #Biostatistics #Research"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
